==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Circa 1959, [[MIT]] {{w|Tech Model Railroad Club}}.<ref>{{w|Peter Samson}}, [http://www.gricer.com/tmrc/dictionary1959.html AN ABRIDGED DICTIONARY of the TMRC LANGUAGE] June 1959 (with 2005 commentary)</ref> {{unk.|lang=en}} origin; possibly from Cruft Hall, built in 1915 as a gift from a donor named Harriet Otis Cruft.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thecrimson.com/article/1916/3/10/bronze-tablet-erected-in-cruft-memorial/|title=Bronze Tablet Erected in Cruft Memorial Laboratory|website=thecrimson.com|accessdate=26 November 2014}}</ref> Cruft Hall was the radar laboratory of [[w:Harvard|Harvard]]'s physics department during the Second World War, which contained much old and unused technical equipment. Possibly {{blend|crust|fluff|nocap=1}}, both of which suggest things that are obsolete and superfluous, or influenced by {{m|en|crud}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|ʌft|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{context|computing|informal|lang=en}} Anything old or of inferior quality.
# {{context|computing|informal|lang=en}} Redundant, old or improperly written [[code]], especially that which accumulates over time; [[clutter]].

